{"name":"Almond oil / castor oil","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":54,"casual":{"lead":["Sweet almond oil and castor oil are massaged around the eyes by people hoping to moisturize thin skin, soften fine lines and reduce the look of dark circles for a smoother, less shadowed under-eye. Both are common cosmetic ingredients.","Both oils are emollients. Almond oil is mostly monounsaturated fat, mainly oleic acid, with vitamin E; castor oil is about 90 percent ricinoleate and is used as a moisturizer in creams. Proponents credit them with nourishing the skin. No clinical trial of either oil around the eyes was found."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People warm a drop or two between the fingers and gently massage it under the eyes at night, sometimes mixing the two oils, and judge the result over weeks."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Almonds can cause allergy, from oral allergy syndrome to anaphylaxis, though reference sources do not address the oil specifically. Bitter almond oil is a different product containing benzaldehyde. Oils near the eye can cause irritation or blurred vision, so people keep them off the eye itself."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Extraction and characterization of exopolysaccharides from Lactiplantibacillus plantarum strain PRK7 and PRK 11, and evaluation of their antioxidant, emulsion, and antibiofilm activities.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37182634/","source":"International journal of biological macromolecules","year":2023,"cites":12,"kind":null},{"title":"Effect of aromatherapy massage with lavender essential oil on sleep quality, pain, and mental and psychiatric disorders among breast cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy: a randomized controlled trial.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40653563/","source":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","year":2025,"cites":null,"kind":"Randomized 